# QUESTION 1 — DOES READER-SIDE CURATION SCALE PAST ONE PERSON'S ATTENTION?

## 1.1 Gonçalves, Perra & Vespignani 2011 (the source claim)

**URL:** https://journals.plos.org/plosone/article?id=10.1371/journal.pone.0022656 · DOI 10.1371/journal.pone.0022656 · PLoS ONE 6(8):e22656 · **live fetch, no Wayback needed**

Abstract, verbatim:

> "Microblogging and mobile devices appear to augment human social capabilities, which raises the question whether they remove cognitive or biological constraints on human communication. In this paper we analyze a dataset of Twitter conversations collected across six months involving 1.7 million individuals and test the theoretical cognitive limit on the number of stable social relationships known as Dunbar's number. We find that the data are in agreement with Dunbar's result; users can entertain a maximum of 100–200 stable relationships. Thus, the 'economy of attention' is limited in the online world by cognitive and biological constraints as predicted by Dunbar's theory. We propose a simple model for users' behavior that includes finite priority queuing and time resources that reproduces the observed social behavior."

Hard numbers:
- **380 million tweets**, **25 million conversations**, **1.7 million individuals**, **six months**.
- The measured quantity is not "number of friends" but **average weight per outgoing connection**, which "reaches a maximum between 100 and 200 friends."
- Conclusion: the model "offers support to Dunbar's hypothesis of a biological limit to the number of relationships than can be simultaneously maintained by a single individual."

**Confidence: HIGH** (full open-access text read directly).

**Load-bearing caveat, mine not theirs:** the ceiling is an *inflection in per-tie investment*, not a hard cap on tie count. Users in the dataset had far more than 200 followees; what saturates is attention per tie. Dunbar himself (below) calls this study out as not sampling "conventional everyday social communities in any meaningful sense."

## 1.2 Dunbar 2016 — the direct online test

**URL:** https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC4736918 (PMC4736918) · DOI 10.1098/rsos.150292 · R. Soc. Open Sci. 3:150292. *royalsocietypublishing.org returned 403; Europe PMC full text used.*

Method: two UK national stratified random samples via OnePoll — **Sample 1 = 2000 adults** (regular social media users, mean age 39, 45.2% male); **Sample 2 = 1375 adults** (full-time professionals, mean age 37.4, 39.1% male). Friend counts collected on 14- and 16-point categorical scales, 0 to 1000+.

Verbatim results:

> "The mean number of friends is 155.2 and 182.8 in the two samples, respectively. Neither of these differs significantly from the value of 150 (95% CI = 100–200) predicted by the SBH [18] (z=0.20, p=0.842; z=1.29, p=0.198, respectively)."

> "On average, respondents in Sample 1 considered that only 27.6% of their Facebook friends could be considered 'genuine' (i.e. close) friends, with a strong modal value between 0 and 10%."

> "The mean values are 4.1 and 13.6, respectively, for the support clique (friends on whom you would depend for emotional/social support in times of crisis) and sympathy group (close friends)."

> "The second main finding is that the two samples provide a direct test of whether online SNSs allow individuals to have larger social networks than is possible offline because SNSs allow one or more of the constraints that limit offline social network size to be circumvented. The results clearly suggest that they do not."

> "only 14.2% of respondents in Sample 1 listed more than 300 friends (i.e. significantly more than the average offline network) and only 13.4% of those in Sample 2 did so."

> "Respondents who had unusually large networks did not increase the numbers of close friendships they had, but rather added more loosely defined acquaintances into their friendship circle simply because most social media sites do not allow one to differentiate between these layers."

Also verbatim, Dunbar's own criticism of the Gonçalves-type evidence:

> "Two attempts to examine the size of extended online social networks with seriously large samples have considered communities formed among twitterati (i.e. followers of a particular twitter account) [52] and scientific email communities [53]. Both claimed evidence for a natural community size between 100 and 200, but neither of these can really be considered conventional everyday social communities in any meaningful sense."

Layer canon stated verbatim: "In humans, these layers have values that approximate 5, 15, 50 and 150, and extend beyond this in at least two further layers to 500 and 1500."

**Confidence: HIGH** on what the paper says. **Confidence MEDIUM-LOW on the finding's strength** — self-reported friend counts on a coarse categorical scale from a commercial polling panel, commissioned by a biscuit company's PR agency ("the WildCard agency on behalf of the Thomas J. Fudge's company" — verbatim from Methods). That provenance is in the paper itself.

## 1.3 The refutation — Lindenfors, Wartel & Lind 2021

**URL:** https://europepmc.org/articles/PMC8103230 (PMC8103230) · DOI 10.1098/rsbl.2021.0158 · Biol. Lett. 17:20210158. *403 direct; Europe PMC used.*

Abstract, verbatim:

> "A widespread and popular belief posits that humans possess a cognitive capacity that is limited to keeping track of and maintaining stable relationships with approximately 150 people. This influential number, 'Dunbar's number', originates from an extrapolation of a regression line describing the relationship between relative neocortex size and group size in primates. Here, we test if there is statistical support for this idea. Our analyses on complementary datasets using different methods yield wildly different numbers. Bayesian and generalized least-squares phylogenetic methods generate approximations of average group sizes between 69–109 and 16–42, respectively. However, enormous 95% confidence intervals (4–520 and 2–336, respectively) imply that specifying any one number is futile. A cognitive limit on human group size cannot be derived in this manner."

Results, verbatim:

> "Our results (table 1) reveal that estimates of expected human group sizes vary depending on method and variable choice (Bayesian approximations between 69.2 and 108.6 and GLE approximations between 16.4 and 42.0). Note that these estimates (as was true for Dunbar's original estimated group size) are averages, not estimates of upper bounds. If an upper constraint from this type of statistical reasoning was to be determined, a better approach would be to specify the upper boundary of the 95% confidence interval. As is shown in table 1, however, 95% confidence intervals yield enormous variation in their estimates, 3.8–520.0 and 2.1–336.3, respectively, and thus indicate upper limits far exceeding 150 in almost all cases."

Table 1 rows recovered: `group size ~ neocortex + rest of brain (n = 71)` → **69.2 [3.8, 292.0]**; `group size ~ neocortex/rest of brain (n = 71)` → **108.6 [4.6, 520.0]**.

Discussion, verbatim:

> "Thus, ecological research on primate sociality, the uniqueness of human thinking and empirical observations all indicate that there is no hard cognitive limit on human sociality. Our reanalysis provides the last piece of evidence needed to disregard Dunbar's number."

> "It is our hope, though perhaps futile, that this study will put an end to the use of 'Dunbar's number' within science and in popular media. 'Dunbar's number' is a concept with limited theoretical foundation lacking empirical support."

They also quote de Ruiter et al. verbatim:

> "Dunbar's assumption that the evolution of human brain physiology corresponds with a limit in our capacity to maintain relationships ignores the cultural mechanisms, practices, and social structures that humans develop to counter potential deficiencies."

**Confidence: HIGH** on text. **Scope caveat that matters for a design brief:** Lindenfors et al. attack *the derivation* (the primate-neocortex regression), not the *behavioral* measurements. The Twitter/phone/Facebook studies measure a real saturation in observed communication effort; Lindenfors shows that number cannot be justified as a *cognitive constant* extrapolated from primate brains. These two literatures do not actually collide head-on. **Confidence MEDIUM** on that reading being the consensus one.

## 1.4 Turnover — the finding that most matters here

### Saramäki et al., "The persistence of social signatures in human communication"
**URL:** https://arxiv.org/abs/1204.5602 (ar5iv full text: https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1204.5602) · published PNAS 111(3):942–947, 2014.

- **24 participants** (12M/12F), **18 months**, tracked across the school→university/work transition, mobile call records plus surveys.
- Network overlap between successive intervals, verbatim: **"⟨J(I₁,I₂)⟩=0.22±0.09 and ⟨J(I₂,I₃)⟩=0.27±0.09"** for whole networks; **"0.36±0.13"** and **"0.44±0.10"** for the top-20 contacts.
- Signature stability: **"82%±12%"** of cross-individual signature distances exceeded the individual's own self-distance across intervals.

**Reading:** the *shape* of attention allocation is a personal constant; the *identities* filling the slots churn hard — roughly three quarters of alters turn over in 18 months, and even the top 20 slots turn over ~55–65%.

**Confidence: HIGH** on numbers, **MEDIUM** on generality — n=24, and a life-transition cohort, which maximizes turnover.

### Miritello et al., "Limited communication capacity unveils strategies for human interaction"
**URL:** https://arxiv.org/abs/1304.1979 (ar5iv: https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1304.1979) · Sci. Rep. 3:1950, 2013.

- **~20 million people**, **19 months**, **~700 million ties** (mobile CDRs).
- Verbatim: **"individuals exhibit a finite communication capacity, which limits the number of ties they can maintain active."**
- Verbatim: **"on average people activate/deactivate about 8 (reciprocated) ties in a period of 7 months"**; **"20% of users in our database activate/deactivate more than 15 ties in that period"**.
- Verbatim: **"average time between tie communication events is ⟨δt_ij⟩ = 14 days (with σ = 18 days)"**.
- Social persistence **"around 75%"** over seven months.
- Verbatim: **"men have an overall higher communication capacity than women and that this capacity decreases gradually for both sexes over the lifespan"** (ages 16–70).

**Confidence: HIGH.** This is the largest-N support for a capacity constraint anywhere in this set, and it is the one that frames the constraint as *throughput* rather than *inventory* — which is the framing that generalizes to feeds.

**NOT REACHED:** Miritello et al., "Time as a limited resource: Communication strategy in mobile phone networks," *Social Networks* 35(1), 2013 — arXiv listing not surfaced by my queries; only the Sci. Rep. companion was retrieved.

### Hodas & Lerman — the attention-division law
**URL:** https://arxiv.org/abs/1205.2736 (ar5iv: https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1205.2736) · "How Visibility and Divided Attention Constrain Social Contagion" (published as Sci. Rep. 4:4343, 2014).

Verbatim: **"Figure 3 shows that this probability decays with the number of friends, denoted nf. The solid line is the fitted function 𝒫nf = 0.22 nf^0.21/(nf+0.53)."** And: **"the more friends a user has, the more this problem is exacerbated, and the probability to respond to any individual friend's tweet is proportionally reduced."**

**Confidence: MEDIUM-HIGH** — the functional form was read out of the ar5iv render; the exponents are as printed but I did not independently verify against the published Sci. Rep. version.

**This is the cleanest quantitative statement of the scaling question you asked.** Per-source responsiveness falls roughly as nf^−0.79 — near-inverse. Aggregate attention is conserved; adding sources dilutes, it does not accumulate.

## 1.5 Layer structure at online scale (Arnaboldi / Passarella / Conti / Dunbar)

**URL:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2206.02422 (ar5iv: https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2206.02422) — "Ego Network Structure in Online Social Networks and its Impact on Information Diffusion."

- **Facebook: 91,347 ego networks. Twitter: 394,238 ego networks.**
- Twitter layers: **C1 = 1.66 · C2 = 5.06 · C3 = 12.87 · C4 = 32.66 · C5 (active network) = 97.47**
- Facebook layers: **C1 = 4.70 · C2 = 15.31 · C3 = 44.77 · C4 = 132.50**
- Verbatim: **"the ratio between the size of adjacent circles appears to be a constant with a value around 3"**
- Verbatim: **"The active network size coincides with the Dunbar's number… for a total of about 150 members."**

**Confidence: MEDIUM-HIGH.** Numbers are as extracted from the ar5iv render. Note the Twitter active network measures **97.47**, meaningfully below 150 — the "coincides with Dunbar's number" gloss is the authors' framing, not a tight fit.

**PARTIALLY REACHED:** the companion paper https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.13980 ("The Structure of Online Social Networks Mirror Those in the Offline World," Dunbar et al.) redirected ar5iv → arxiv.org/abs and I did not re-fetch. Its layer numbers are believed consistent with the above but **NOT INDEPENDENTLY CONFIRMED**.

## 1.6 GROUP memberships (not dyads) — the part you specifically asked for

This literature is much thinner than the dyadic one. Two solid sources:

### Hamilton et al., "All Who Wander: On the Prevalence and Characteristics of Multi-community Engagement" (WWW 2015)
**URL:** https://arxiv.org/abs/1503.01180 (ar5iv: https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1503.01180)

- Reddit: **157K users with 50+ posts** who first posted Jan 2008–Jan 2012; **76.6M posts**.
- **Average communities per user: 28.85; median: 26.00.** (Average posts/user 152.04, median 89.)
- DBLP comparison: **average venues 38.08, median 34.00**.
- Verbatim: **"Redditors post to 5 communities in their first 10 posts and then post to 2.5 new communities every 10 posts."**
- Verbatim: **"Users post to around 5 communities every 10 posts"**, with **"only around 2.5 of them are ones that they have ever posted to."**
- Abandonment, verbatim: **"Users rack up more abandoned communities than return engagements"** — communities posted to exactly once outnumber those posted to twice or more.

**Confidence: HIGH** on the numbers; **MEDIUM** on interpretation — this is a *cumulative lifetime* count (~29 communities), and the *concurrent active* count implied by the window statistics is **~5 per 10 posts, of which only ~2.5 are repeats**. That concurrent figure (≈2–5) is the design-relevant one, and it is roughly an order of magnitude below the dyadic-tie ceiling.

### Hamilton et al., "Loyalty in Online Communities" (ICWSM 2017)
**URL:** https://arxiv.org/abs/1703.03386 (ar5iv: https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/1703.03386)

- All Reddit 2014: **"approximately 10⁸ comments made by 10⁷ users in 10⁴ communities."**
- **242 communities studied; 177,593 loyal users and 1,989,530 vagrants** — an ~11:1 vagrant:loyal ratio.
- Loyal and vagrant users **"contribute about 10% each of all of the comments made to a community."**
- Median month-over-month loyalty rate across **1,440 communities: 60.7%**.
- Verbatim framing: **"When users have the choice to engage with a variety of different communities, they often become loyal to just one, focusing on that community at the expense of others."**

**Confidence: HIGH** on numbers as extracted. **The 60.7% median monthly retention implies ~40% monthly membership churn** — group memberships appear to churn *faster* than dyadic ties (75% seven-month persistence in Miritello).

### **NOT REACHED / NEGATIVE RESULTS — group-count ceilings**
- **No study measuring a ceiling on the number of groups/channels/servers a person can concurrently sustain was found.** arXiv queries `"community membership" AND turnover AND online`, `"multicommunity" AND engagement`, and `abs:"number of communities" AND users AND online` returned **zero relevant hits**. Crossref queries on feed/algorithm choice returned nothing on point.
- No Discord-server-count, Slack-workspace-count, or Facebook-Groups-count study reached.
- **This is a genuine gap in the literature, not a gap in my search coverage** (three independent query framings, all empty). Treat "how many groups can one person hold" as **unmeasured**. The Reddit ~29-lifetime / ~2.5-repeat-per-window figures are the closest available proxy, and they come from one platform and one research group.

