# QUESTION 2 — DEFAULTS AND CHOICE OVERLOAD

## 2.1 The defaults meta-analysis — Jachimowicz, Duncan, Weber & Johnson 2019

**URL/DOI:** https://doi.org/10.1017/bpp.2018.43 · *Behavioural Public Policy* 3(2):159–186. Cambridge Core page returned HTTP 500 to WebFetch; **the full abstract was recovered verbatim from the Crossref JATS record**.

Verbatim:

> "To answer these questions, we conduct a literature search and meta-analysis of the 58 default studies (pooled *n* = 73,675) that fit our criteria. While our analysis reveals a considerable influence of defaults (*d* = 0.68, 95% confidence interval = 0.53–0.83), we also discover substantial variation: the majority of default studies find positive effects, but several do not find a significant effect, and two even demonstrate negative effects."

> "Our analysis reveals two factors that partially account for the variability in defaults' effectiveness. First, we find that defaults in consumer domains are more effective and in environmental domains are less effective. Second, we find that defaults are more effective when they operate through **endorsement** (defaults that are seen as conveying what the choice architect thinks the choice-maker should do) or **endowment** (defaults that are seen as reflecting the status quo)."

**Headline: d = 0.68, 95% CI [0.53, 0.83], k = 58, pooled n = 73,675.**

**Confidence: HIGH** on the numbers (Crossref-deposited publisher abstract). **Confidence MEDIUM** that I have the full moderator picture — I did not reach the body text, so the per-moderator effect sizes and any publication-bias correction are **NOT REACHED**.

## 2.2 Johnson & Goldstein 2003, "Do Defaults Save Lives?"

**URL:** https://www.dangoldstein.com/papers/DefaultsScience.pdf (author-hosted, live) · *Science* 302:1338–1339 · DOI 10.1126/science.1091721.

Numbers as printed in the figures and text:
- **Online experiment: 161 respondents.** Consent rates: **opt-in 42%, opt-out 82%, neutral (mandated choice) 79%.**
- **Country-level effective consent rates**, opt-in (gold) vs opt-out (blue): opt-in values **4.25, 17.17, …**; opt-out values **99.98, 98, 99.91, 99.97, 99.5, 99.64, 85.9**.
- Verbatim: **"the four opt-in countries (gold) had lower rates than the six opt-out countries (blue). The two distributions have no overlap, and nearly 60 percentage points separate the two groups."**
- Verbatim on *actual* donations (not just consent): **"When donation is the default, there is a 16.3% (P < 0.02) increase in donation, increasing the donor rate from 14.1 to 16.4 million"** [per million population]. Gimbel et al. estimate, verbatim: **"an increase in the rate from 10.8 to 16.9, a 56.5% increase."**
- The Netherlands natural experiment, verbatim: a 1998 national registry plus **"a mass mailing (of more than 12 million letters in a country of 15.8 million) asking citizens to register, which failed to change the effective consent rate."**

**Confidence: HIGH.** Note the load-bearing gap the paper itself flags: the ~60-point default effect is on *registration*, while the effect on *actual transplants* is 16.3%. Registration is cheap; behavior is not. That gap is the direct analogue of "subscribed to a feed" vs "reads the feed."

## 2.3 CHOICE OVERLOAD — the near-zero meta-analysis

**Scheibehenne, Greifeneder & Todd 2010, "Can There Ever Be Too Many Options? A Meta-Analytic Review of Choice Overload,"** *Journal of Consumer Research* 37(3):409–425 · DOI 10.1086/651235.
**URL:** https://www.scheibehenne.de/ScheibehenneGreifenederTodd2010.pdf (author-hosted; live fetch succeeded. Wayback capture also confirmed: http://web.archive.org/web/20260820041254/https://www.scheibehenne.de/ScheibehenneGreifenederTodd2010.pdf — **capture date 2026-08-20**)

Abstract, verbatim:

> "In a meta-analysis of 63 conditions from 50 published and unpublished experiments (N = 5,036), we found a mean effect size of virtually zero but considerable variance between studies. While further analyses indicated several potentially important preconditions for choice overload, no sufficient conditions could be identified."

Results section, verbatim:

> "The mean effect size of choice overload across all 63 data points according to equation 1 is **D = 0.02 (95% confidence interval [CI95] −0.09 to 0.12)**. The between-study variance is **τ² = 0.12 (CI95 −0.08 to 0.25)**… For the data set on hand, **Q(62) = 192 (p < .001)**… The presumably more informative I²-statistic… yields **I² = 68%**, also implying medium to high heterogeneity."

Robustness, verbatim:

> "If the data set was trimmed by 20% by excluding the six studies with the highest effect sizes and the six studies with the lowest, **D_trimmed = 0.001 (CI95 −0.08 to 0.07)**. The unexplained variance in the trimmed data set reduced to **I² = 22%**, indicating little heterogeneity, which hints that most of the heterogeneity (but not the small mean effect size) in the original data set might be due to a few studies reporting large effect sizes in both directions."

And: **"The overall mean effect size across 63 conditions from 50 experiments in our meta-analysis was virtually zero."**

**Confidence: HIGH** — read directly out of the PDF text layer.

**This is the strongest negative result in the brief.** Defaults are a large, robust effect (d = 0.68). Choice overload, as an average main effect, is *not an effect at all* (D = 0.02, CI straddling zero, and 0.001 after trimming). Any design argument of the form "too many feed options will paralyze users" has **no meta-analytic support**. The correct opposing argument is the default effect, which is separately and much better evidenced.

## 2.4 Iyengar & Lepper 2000 — the study the near-zero meta-analysis dissolves

**DOI:** 10.1037/0022-3514.79.6.995 · *JPSP* 79(6):995–1006.
**URL:** https://faculty.washington.edu/jdb/345/345%20Articles/Iyengar%20%26%20Lepper%20(2000).pdf (course-hosted PDF, live)

Study 1 (the jam study), verbatim fragments:
- Stimulus: Wilkin & Sons jams; **6 flavors (limited) vs 24 flavors (extensive)**; price **$4–6**; all approachers got a **$1-discount coupon**, redeemable over one week.
- Booth stopping: **"selection display of jams, 60% (145) actually stopped at the booth. In contrast, of the 260 customers who passed the limited-selection display of jams, only 40% (104) stopped."** (i.e. the *large* assortment drew more traffic)
- Purchase: **"Nearly 30% (31) of the consumers in the limited-choice condition subsequently purchased a jar of Wilkin & Sons jam; in contrast, only 3%…"** — **χ²(1, N = 249) = 32.34, p < .0001.**
- Study 3 (essay): **"only 60% chose to complete the assignment"** in the extensive-choice condition.

**Confidence: HIGH** on numbers. **Note:** the purchase-rate contrast rests on **31 purchases in one condition versus roughly 4 in the other** — a very small absolute cell, which is consistent with Scheibehenne et al. finding no replicable average effect.

**NOT REACHED:** Chernev, Böckenholt & Goodman 2015, "Choice overload: A conceptual review and meta-analysis" (*J. Consumer Psychology*), which reports a *nonzero* effect conditional on four moderators and is the standard rebuttal to Scheibehenne. Crossref returned no abstract and I did not obtain full text. **Its absence materially weakens the "choice overload is nothing" reading** — treat Scheibehenne's null as the unconditional mean, not as a settled verdict.

## 2.5 Applied to FEED / ALGORITHM selection

### Fukuyama et al., Stanford Working Group on Platform Scale (Nov 2020)
**URL (landing):** https://cyber.fsi.stanford.edu/publication/report-working-group-platform-scale
**URL (PDF, fetched):** https://fsi-live.s3.us-west-1.amazonaws.com/s3fs-public/platform_scale_whitepaper_-cpc-pacs.pdf
**Companion cited by Keller:** https://fsi-live.s3.us-west-1.amazonaws.com/s3fs-public/cpc-middleware_ff_v2.pdf

The report **explicitly anticipates the default problem and proposes to legislate against it.** Verbatim:

> "we consider it necessary to mandate that dominant search engines and social media companies allow users to choose among third-party filters. Moreover, the platforms might be compelled to alert users to the option of installing middleware and to require users to explicitly opt out of middleware use. **Middleware can serve its intended purpose only if it is used widely, and a default option that allows users to never consider installing middleware would severely limit its uptake.**"

Also verbatim:

> "Platforms could also offer their own middleware, on the condition that they do not favor their own product over those provided by third parties **or make their own middleware a default choice**."

> "a business model for middleware providers must be sufficiently attractive to induce an adequate supply. The most logical approach would be to establish revenue sharing arrangements between the dominant platforms and the third-party providers of middleware."

Epigraph, Jack Dorsey, 8 Aug 2020, verbatim:

> "We need to open up and be transparent around how our algorithms work and how they're used, and maybe even enable people to choose their own algorithms to rank the content or to create their own algorithms, to rank it."

**Confidence: HIGH** (direct PDF text).

### Daphne Keller, "The Future of Platform Power: Making Middleware Work"
**URL:** https://www.journalofdemocracy.org/articles/the-future-of-platform-power-making-middleware-work/ · *Journal of Democracy* 32(3), July 2021, pp. 168–172.

Her four named problems, verbatim, with the on-point skepticism:

> "Second, how is everyone going to get paid? **Without a profit motive for middleware providers, the magic will not happen, or it will not happen at large enough scale.** Something about business models—or, at a minimum, the distribution of ads and ad revenue—will have to change."

> "Facebook deploys tens of thousands of people to moderate user content in dozens of languages. It relies on proprietary machine-learning and other automated tools, developed at enormous cost. **We cannot expect comparable investment from a diverse ecosystem of middleware providers.** And while most providers presumably will not handle as much content as Facebook does, they will still need to respond swiftly to novel and unpredictable material from unexpected sources. **Unless middleware services can do this, the value they provide will be limited, as will users' incentives to choose them over curation by the platforms themselves.**"

*(That last clause is the closest thing in Keller to a direct claim that users will not pick alternative feeds. She frames it as a supply-side consequence — bad curation → no reason to switch — not as user apathy.)*

The historical precedent, verbatim:

> "Similar projects—such as the well-intentioned Internet Content Rating Association (ICRA), which failed in the early 2000s—have foundered on moderation costs before. ICRA's mission, like Fukuyama's, was to move control over content away from centralized gatekeepers, out closer to the edges of the network. The plan was to do so in part using customized browser settings, and in part by letting users choose from an ecosystem of trusted third-party curators. A user might subscribe to a block-list from her church… Or she might block most sexual content, but use an add-list from Planned Parenthood to preserve access to health information."

> "**ICRA's third-party curators never showed up. Assessing all that content was hard and thankless, even when the internet was much smaller.** Of course, many things were different then—and, significantly, ICRA offered curators no way to make money. But its demise is a sobering reminder that curation costs matter."

The privacy ceiling on what a third-party curator can even see, verbatim:

> "On Facebook, they may miss out on content from over 80 percent of users (though good data on this are hard to come by). Even on Twitter, which is generally more public-facing, about 13 percent of posts are private. **A vendor lacking access to such large shares of content will have trouble offering useful curation, and even more trouble competing with Facebook itself**, which can see, assess, rank, and label everything in a user's feed."

Her footnote 4 gives the underlying figure verbatim: Bailey et al., NBER WP 25843 — **"in 335 million user-weeks of data, the average user had 53.9 friends with 'public' statuses out of 328 total friends."** (https://www.nber.org/papers/w25843)

The monoculture risk, verbatim:

> "More troublingly, there is a risk that this model will end up spreading not just information, but major platforms' assessments of content. This could reimpose the very speech monoculture from which middleware was supposed to save us."

And her framing device, verbatim:

> "Every year, in my platform-regulation class, I draw a Venn diagram on the board with three interlocking circles: privacy, speech, and competition. Then we identify all the issues that fall at the intersection of two or more circles. Interoperability, including for content-moderation purposes, is always smack in the middle."

**Confidence: HIGH** (full article text extracted from the live page).

### The one piece of MEASURED uptake data — Bluesky custom feeds
**URL:** https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17571 (ar5iv: https://ar5iv.labs.arxiv.org/html/2405.17571) — Balduf et al., "Bluesky: Network Topology, Polarization, and Algorithmic Curation."

Verbatim numbers:
- **"39,639 feeds have been created by 18,352 active users"**
- **"only 139 thousand out of 5 million users liked at least one feed"** — **≈2.8%**
- **"139,033 Users have used this feature and liked feeds 295,902 times"**
- **"On average, feeds only attracted two likes"**
- Authors' conclusion, verbatim: **"while a large number of custom feeds have been created, users' uptake of them appears to be limited"**

**Confidence: MEDIUM-HIGH** on the numbers; **MEDIUM** on the inference. **Important caveat the paper itself does not resolve:** *liking* a feed is not *subscribing to* or *reading* one. The paper does not report what fraction of users actually pin or read a non-default feed, so 2.8% is a **lower bound on engagement with a weaker signal**, not a measured switching rate. The paper explicitly does not state the default-retention fraction.

**This is the only empirical uptake number I reached for reader-side algorithmic choice on a live platform, on the one platform purpose-built to make it easy.** That it is ~3% on a *self-selected, technically sophisticated early-adopter population* is the strongest available evidence bearing on your question — and it is thin, single-source, single-platform evidence.

### Hogg & DiResta, "Enabling a Thriving Middleware Market," Tech Policy Press, 11 Apr 2025
**URL:** https://www.techpolicy.press/enabling-a-thriving-middleware-market/ (Wayback capture also exists: 20250413162931)

Verbatim: **"it is unclear how many users are aware of them. Broad user adoption of middleware will likely require some education."** And: **"funding models for protocol-based social media remain an open question."**
The piece contains **no numerical adoption data**.

**Confidence: MEDIUM** — extracted via WebFetch summary with quoted fragments; I did not read the raw page.

---

## NOT REACHED — the honest list

1. **Chernev, Böckenholt & Goodman 2015** choice-overload meta-analysis (the moderated, nonzero counterpart to Scheibehenne). Materially weakens the "choice overload is nothing" claim.
2. **Jachimowicz et al. body text** — only the publisher abstract via Crossref. Per-moderator effect sizes, publication-bias analysis, and the study list are unread.
3. **Miritello et al., *Social Networks* 35(1) 2013**, "Time as a limited resource."
4. **Any measured ceiling on concurrent group/community memberships.** Three independent query framings returned nothing. I believe this is a real hole in the literature.
5. **Twitter/X "Latest Tweets" chronological-toggle adoption rates** — no source found; Twitter has never published it.
6. **Keller stating flatly that users won't choose alternative feeds.** Her published skepticism is supply-side (cost, revenue, privacy-limited data access), not demand-side apathy. The strongest demand-side sentence she wrote is the conditional one quoted in 2.5. **Do not attribute a stronger claim to her than that.**
7. **Arnaboldi/Dunbar 2205.13980** — ar5iv redirected; layer figures unconfirmed from that specific paper (confirmed from its companion 2206.02422).
8. **royalsocietypublishing.org is 403 to WebFetch** — both Royal Society papers required the Europe PMC route.

## ONE INSTRUMENT WARNING

Every "Dunbar ceiling online" number in this brief traces back through a small number of authors — Dunbar co-authors or cites nearly all of them (Gonçalves 2011 is cited *and dismissed* by Dunbar 2016; Arnaboldi's layer papers are co-authored with Dunbar). The refutation (Lindenfors) comes from outside that circle and attacks the derivation rather than the measurements. **Treat "≈150" as a well-replicated empirical regularity within one research program and a poorly-justified cognitive constant** — those are different claims, and the literature routinely elides them.
